// Foundation's lint vocabulary: the `Lint` construct family a `linter.kira`
// configures a package's lints with.
//
// A lint entry names one check, how loudly it speaks, and whether it runs. It
// goes in `linter.kira` at the package root, and every member carries a written
// type because a construct-backed form requires them:
//
//     construct QualifiedVariant() extends Lint {
//         let code: KiraError = .KLINT001
//         let level: LintLevel = .Allow
//     }
//
// The package root, beside `package.kira` — not under `app/`. What governs a
// whole package belongs next to what the package is called and what it depends
// on, where a reader looks for it. Assembly adds it to the package's modules
// (`kira-program-graph`), so it is compiled with everything else and the
// entries in it are declarations the collector reads like any other.
//
// `Lint` is an ordinary construct family declared here in Kira, exactly as
// `Test` is. The compiler knows nothing about the name: `LintRunner` selects
// entries with `d.family == "Lint"`, a string compare against the family a
// declaration was written in, so a library could declare its own family and its
// own runner and get the same treatment with no compiler change.
//
// # Why the entries are data and the checks are not
//
// A lint runs while the program is being *compiled*, and the only thing that
// runs then is a `comptime macro`'s `expand` body. A `Lint` declaration is an
// ordinary declaration — the runner can read it, but cannot call into it, so a
// check written as a member here would never execute.
//
// So the split is: this file and a package's `linter.kira` say *which* lints
// run and how, and `LintRunner.kira` holds what each one actually looks for.
// Adding a lint is a branch in the runner plus an entry in a `linter.kira` —
// both ordinary Kira, neither a compiler release.

// What a lint does, as one value.
//
// Whether a lint runs and how loudly it speaks are the same question, so they
// are one field. They used to be two — an `enabled: Bool` beside a
// `severity: String` — which made `enabled = false, severity = "error"` a state
// that parses, reads as emphatic, and means nothing. Three levels, one of which
// is off.
//
// The same three the toolchain already had: `kira-linter`'s `LintLevel` is
// `Allow | Warn | Deny`, and only the surface a package writes disagreed.
enum LintLevel {
    // The lint does not run. Not "runs and is ignored": nothing is walked, so
    // an allowed lint costs nothing.
    Allow
    // Reported, and the build carries on. The default for almost everything: a
    // lint has an opinion about code that already compiles.
    Warn
    // Reported, and the build fails.
    Deny
}

// The level an entry's `level` text names, defaulting to a warning.
//
// Read from text because a member's value arrives at a macro as the source it
// was written with — `.Warn`, `LintLevel.Warn`, both — so the spelling is
// matched rather than the value compared. A misspelling warns rather than
// denying: a typo in configuration should not be what fails a build.
function lintLevelFromText(text: borrow String) -> LintLevel {
    if text.contains("Allow") {
        return .Allow
    }
    if text.contains("Deny") {
        return .Deny
    }
    return .Warn
}

// Which family of judgement a lint belongs to, and so how eagerly it runs.
//
// Severity says what a finding *costs*; a group says whether the lint is asked
// in the first place. The two are independent: a pedantic lint can be denied by
// a package that opted into pedantry, and a correctness lint can be allowed by
// a package that disagrees with it.
//
// The default set is everything except `Pedantic` and `Restriction`, which is
// what makes `kira lint` useful without configuration and quiet enough to keep
// reading.
enum LintGroup {
    // Almost certainly a bug. On by default, and the group a lint belongs in
    // only when being wrong about it would be a defect rather than a taste.
    Correctness
    // How the language is written here. On by default: mechanical, uncontested
    // rewrites like a counted `while` that a `for` says.
    Style
    // Doing in several steps what one step does. On by default.
    Complexity
    // Work the program does not need to do. On by default.
    Perf
    // Right, and noisy. Off by default: a lint lands here when it is correct
    // about what it finds and finds things a reader may reasonably keep —
    // including code a generator wrote and nobody can rewrite.
    Pedantic
    // A rule a project chooses rather than a defect anyone would recognise: a
    // line ceiling, a banned construct. Never on by default, because it is a
    // policy and not an opinion about correctness.
    Restriction
}

// One lint's configuration, as a `linter.kira` writes it.
//
// An override, not a switch. Every lint has a level before any package says
// anything, so a `linter.kira` states what *differs* — and a package with no
// `linter.kira` at all gets the standard set rather than silence. Silence used
// to mean "nothing was asked for", which reads exactly like "nothing was
// found".
//
// `code` is the only required member. It is what an entry is matched by: the
// declaration's own name is a label for a reader, and matching on it made the
// name load-bearing, so renaming an entry silently turned its lint off.
//
// `limit` is for the lints that measure something. Left out it is 0, which a
// lint reads as "use my own default" rather than as "allow nothing".
construct Lint {
    // The code this entry configures, as the generated case rather than as
    // text. `KiraError` is written from the same table the compiler emits
    // against, so a typo is a name that does not exist rather than an entry
    // that silently matches nothing — which is what `"KLNT002"` used to be.
    @Required let code: KiraError
    let level: LintLevel = .Warn
    let limit: Int = 0
}

// A package's default level, when it wants one other than the built-in.
//
// One entry, no code: it moves every lint that has no entry of its own. A
// package that wants its lints to fail the build writes `.Deny` here rather
// than repeating it per lint, and a package that wants them quiet during a
// refactor writes `.Allow`.
construct LintDefaults {
    let level: LintLevel = .Warn
    // The groups this package asks for, as one text of comma-separated names.
    //
    // Written as text rather than as a list of cases because a macro reads a
    // member's value back as the source it was written with, and one string is
    // one thing to parse. `"style, pedantic"` adds pedantry to the default set;
    // `"all"` asks for everything, which is what a survey of a codebase wants
    // and what nobody should leave switched on.
    //
    // Empty means the default set: every group except `Pedantic` and
    // `Restriction`.
    let groups: [LintGroup] = []
}
